WEBVTT

00:00:00.381 --> 00:00:02.324
Here's the black ink dot from last time,

00:00:02.725 --> 00:00:04.648
sitting on a pencil line near the bottom

00:00:04.769 --> 00:00:05.530
of a paper strip.

00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:07.657
Stand the strip in a jar with a

00:00:07.698 --> 00:00:10.107
little water, below the line, and put on

00:00:10.148 --> 00:00:10.469
the lid.

00:00:11.173 --> 00:00:13.239
The water slowly soaks up the paper,

00:00:13.762 --> 00:00:15.568
and as it passes the ink, the black

00:00:15.609 --> 00:00:16.492
dot comes apart.

00:00:17.235 --> 00:00:19.098
A blue dye, a pink dye and a

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:21.883
yellow dye end up at three different heights.

00:00:22.706 --> 00:00:24.555
They all started on the same line, and

00:00:24.576 --> 00:00:25.701
they all had the same time.

00:00:26.346 --> 00:00:29.579
So why do mixture components travel different distances

00:00:29.721 --> 00:00:30.483
in chromatography?

00:00:31.377 --> 00:00:33.572
Chromatography always has two parts.

00:00:34.096 --> 00:00:36.530
The paper is the stationary phase, the part

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:37.234
that stays still.

00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:40.092
The water is the mobile phase, the part

00:00:40.113 --> 00:00:40.595
that moves.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.103
It climbs the paper the way a spill

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:45.469
soaks into a paper towel, drawn up into

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.493
the tiny gaps between the paper's fibres.

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:50.501
As it climbs, it carries the dissolved dyes

00:00:50.542 --> 00:00:52.466
with it, but not all at the same

00:00:52.507 --> 00:00:52.747
speed.

00:00:53.646 --> 00:00:54.133
Here's the key.

00:00:54.741 --> 00:00:57.640
Each dye particle keeps switching between two places.

00:00:58.243 --> 00:00:59.686
It can stick to the paper and stay

00:00:59.727 --> 00:00:59.926
put,

00:01:00.549 --> 00:01:02.231
or it can dissolve in the moving water

00:01:02.513 --> 00:01:03.194
and get carried up.

00:01:04.036 --> 00:01:05.816
A dye that's attracted more strongly to the

00:01:05.877 --> 00:01:08.598
paper spends more of its time stuck, so

00:01:08.839 --> 00:01:11.020
it falls behind and travels a shorter distance.

00:01:11.842 --> 00:01:13.485
A dye that dissolves well in the water,

00:01:13.846 --> 00:01:16.010
and sticks only weakly to the paper, spends

00:01:16.051 --> 00:01:18.214
more of its time moving, so it travels

00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:18.555
further.

00:01:19.358 --> 00:01:21.663
Every dye had the same time, so different

00:01:21.704 --> 00:01:23.147
attractions spread them apart.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.579
That's why one black dot becomes three spots.

00:01:27.202 --> 00:01:27.828
Quick prediction.

00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:31.066
Of these three dyes, which one is attracted

00:01:31.288 --> 00:01:32.775
most strongly to the paper?

00:01:33.505 --> 00:01:33.893
Have a think.

00:01:37.756 --> 00:01:39.644
It's the blue one, lowest on the strip,

00:01:39.906 --> 00:01:41.734
because it spent the most time stuck.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.811
To compare spots fairly, chemists use a ratio

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:47.276
called the R F value.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.485
It's the distance the spot moved, divided by

00:01:50.506 --> 00:01:52.250
the distance the solvent moved.

00:01:52.993 --> 00:01:55.384
Both are measured from the baseline, the pencil

00:01:55.405 --> 00:01:56.811
line where the sample started.

00:01:57.535 --> 00:01:59.504
For the spot, measure to its centre.

00:02:00.087 --> 00:02:02.597
For the solvent, measure to the solvent front,

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:04.785
the highest point the solvent reached.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:06.793
A spot can never get ahead of the

00:02:06.854 --> 00:02:09.904
solvent, so R F is always between zero

00:02:10.025 --> 00:02:11.830
and one, and it has no units.

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:13.300
Let's try one.

00:02:13.962 --> 00:02:16.749
A green food colouring separates into two spots,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.032
P and Q.

00:02:18.794 --> 00:02:21.282
The solvent front is eight point zero centimetres

00:02:21.422 --> 00:02:22.365
above the baseline.

00:02:23.207 --> 00:02:26.033
Spot P moved two point four centimetres, and

00:02:26.073 --> 00:02:28.158
spot Q moved six point four.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.310
For P, two point four divided by eight

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.962
point zero gives zero point three zero.

00:02:34.625 --> 00:02:36.730
For Q, six point four divided by eight

00:02:36.771 --> 00:02:39.116
point zero gives zero point eight zero.

00:02:39.881 --> 00:02:42.233
Both answers have two significant figures, like the

00:02:42.254 --> 00:02:42.595
measurements.

00:02:43.615 --> 00:02:44.440
Why a ratio?

00:02:45.226 --> 00:02:47.729
If you'd stopped this run halfway, the front

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.694
would be at four point zero centimetres, P

00:02:50.775 --> 00:02:52.958
at one point two and Q at three

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:53.419
point two.

00:02:54.222 --> 00:02:57.452
Every distance halves, but the ratios stay at

00:02:57.513 --> 00:03:00.001
zero point three zero and zero point eight

00:03:00.042 --> 00:03:00.362
zero.

00:03:01.106 --> 00:03:03.358
Each spot moves a fixed fraction of the

00:03:03.399 --> 00:03:04.202
solvent's distance.

00:03:05.088 --> 00:03:07.578
But that fraction depends on the conditions, the

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:10.229
type of paper, the solvent and the temperature.

00:03:10.812 --> 00:03:12.524
Change any of them, and the R F

00:03:12.545 --> 00:03:13.250
values change.

00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:16.994
That's how chromatography helps identify substances.

00:03:17.718 --> 00:03:20.788
Run known substances, called standards, on the same

00:03:20.849 --> 00:03:23.196
paper, in the same solvent, at the same

00:03:23.298 --> 00:03:23.638
time.

00:03:24.461 --> 00:03:26.604
Here, standard X has an R F of

00:03:26.685 --> 00:03:29.390
zero point three zero, and standard Y has

00:03:29.431 --> 00:03:30.632
zero point eight zero.

00:03:31.194 --> 00:03:34.022
So spot P matches X, and spot Q

00:03:34.264 --> 00:03:34.885
matches Y.

00:03:35.607 --> 00:03:38.252
But a match supports the identity, it doesn't

00:03:38.313 --> 00:03:41.298
prove it, because two different substances can share

00:03:41.359 --> 00:03:42.180
an R F value.

00:03:42.862 --> 00:03:44.966
To be more confident, run them again in

00:03:45.007 --> 00:03:47.232
a different solvent, and check they still match.

00:03:48.278 --> 00:03:50.414
A few details make the results trustworthy.

00:03:51.077 --> 00:03:53.662
Draw the baseline in pencil, because pencil doesn't

00:03:53.683 --> 00:03:55.928
dissolve, but ink would run up the paper

00:03:55.969 --> 00:03:56.148
too.

00:03:56.951 --> 00:03:59.337
Keep the solvent below the baseline, or the

00:03:59.378 --> 00:04:00.861
spots just dissolve into the pool.

00:04:01.685 --> 00:04:03.428
Keep the lid on, so the solvent doesn't

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:05.273
evaporate off the paper as it climbs.

00:04:05.956 --> 00:04:07.740
And mark the solvent front as soon as

00:04:07.781 --> 00:04:09.686
you take the strip out, because it dries

00:04:09.928 --> 00:04:10.468
and disappears.

00:04:10.995 --> 00:04:12.101
Two classic traps.

00:04:12.765 --> 00:04:14.850
Measure from the baseline, not from the bottom

00:04:14.891 --> 00:04:15.511
edge of the paper,

00:04:16.254 --> 00:04:17.797
and to the centre of each spot.

00:04:18.681 --> 00:04:20.428
And put the spot's distance on top.

00:04:21.052 --> 00:04:22.380
If you ever get an R F bigger

00:04:22.421 --> 00:04:23.789
than one, you've flipped it.

00:04:24.677 --> 00:04:26.010
Here's a way to remember the whole idea.

00:04:26.636 --> 00:04:27.699
Spot over front,

00:04:28.262 --> 00:04:29.787
and stickier stays lower.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.817
The spot cannot pass the front.

00:04:32.481 --> 00:04:33.885
And the dye that sticks to the paper

00:04:33.986 --> 00:04:35.992
most has the smallest R F.

00:04:38.705 --> 00:04:39.255
Your turn.

00:04:39.882 --> 00:04:42.351
An unknown food dye is run alongside a

00:04:42.552 --> 00:04:45.382
tartrazine standard, on the same paper in the

00:04:45.442 --> 00:04:46.245
same solvent.

00:04:46.726 --> 00:04:49.368
The solvent front travels ten point zero centimetres,

00:04:49.808 --> 00:04:52.450
and the unknown's spot travels four point zero

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:52.951
centimetres.

00:04:53.671 --> 00:04:56.420
Tartrazine's R F under these conditions is zero

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:57.423
point four zero.

00:04:58.166 --> 00:05:00.892
Calculate the unknown's R F, and evaluate the

00:05:00.913 --> 00:05:02.717
claim that it must be tartrazine.

00:05:03.363 --> 00:05:04.231
Pause here to answer.

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:17.477
R F is four point zero divided by

00:05:17.558 --> 00:05:19.687
ten point zero, which is zero point four

00:05:19.728 --> 00:05:20.029
zero.

00:05:20.652 --> 00:05:23.299
That matches tartrazine under the same conditions, so

00:05:23.581 --> 00:05:24.663
it supports the conclusion.

00:05:25.326 --> 00:05:27.598
But it doesn't prove it, because another dye

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.725
could have the same R F.

00:05:29.711 --> 00:05:31.825
Running both in a second solvent would make

00:05:31.846 --> 00:05:32.489
the case stronger.

00:05:33.294 --> 00:05:35.762
Ten divided by four, two point five, is

00:05:35.803 --> 00:05:36.625
the slip to avoid.

00:05:38.289 --> 00:05:38.671
Last one.

00:05:39.678 --> 00:05:42.385
In a paper chromatogram, the solvent front travels

00:05:42.466 --> 00:05:43.989
twelve point zero centimetres.

00:05:44.552 --> 00:05:47.458
Spot A travels three point zero centimetres, and

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.624
spot B travels nine point zero.

00:05:50.306 --> 00:05:52.867
Calculate the R F of each spot, and

00:05:52.928 --> 00:05:55.909
identify which component is more strongly attracted to

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:56.850
the stationary phase.

00:05:57.591 --> 00:05:58.532
Pause here to answer.

00:06:15.075 --> 00:06:17.426
For A, three point zero over twelve point

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:19.194
zero is zero point two five.

00:06:19.758 --> 00:06:22.090
For B, nine point zero over twelve point

00:06:22.111 --> 00:06:23.961
zero is zero point seven five.

00:06:24.764 --> 00:06:27.412
A is more strongly attracted to the stationary

00:06:27.473 --> 00:06:27.773
phase.

00:06:28.195 --> 00:06:29.939
It spent more time stuck to the paper,

00:06:30.301 --> 00:06:32.405
so it travelled a smaller fraction of the

00:06:32.486 --> 00:06:33.347
solvent's distance.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.205
Choosing B because it went further is the

00:06:36.226 --> 00:06:36.910
slip to avoid.

00:06:38.701 --> 00:06:41.471
So chromatography tells you which substances are in

00:06:41.511 --> 00:06:41.952
a mixture.

00:06:42.576 --> 00:06:44.490
It doesn't tell you how much of each

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:44.873
there is.

00:06:45.618 --> 00:06:47.164
For that, we go back to the sand

00:06:47.264 --> 00:06:49.492
and salt we separated, and we weigh them.

00:06:50.013 --> 00:06:52.176
Next time, we'll work out what fraction of

00:06:52.216 --> 00:06:54.819
a separated mixture came from each component.

00:06:57.628 --> 00:06:58.450
Here are the key notes.

00:06:58.974 --> 00:07:00.521
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:00.562 --> 00:07:00.983
to copy them.
